    {
      "unit_id": "qrm-acceptance-sampling-oc-curve-a47b09353043:p0010:text",
      "course_id": "qrm",
      "document_id": "qrm-acceptance-sampling-oc-curve-a47b09353043",
      "kind": "page_text",
      "text": "Double Sampling Plan\n\nProcess: Take a first sample n1, then:\n   If the number of nonconforming items is ≤ c1, accept the\n       lot.\n   If it is ≥ r1, reject the lot.\n   If it’s between these values, take a second sample of size\n      n2 and combine findings:\n     If total nonconforming items from both samples ≤ c2,\n        accept the lot.\n     If ≥ r2, reject the lot.\nAdvantages: Often results in fewer inspections, on average,\n  especially when product quality is clearly good or bad in\n  the first sample.\nDisadvantages: More complex to execute and administer\n  than single plans.",
      "locator": {
        "page": 10,
